{"records":[{"id":"87pvmycwfm","title":"Full-length three-dimensional structure of the influenza A virus M1 protein and its organization into a matrix layer","description":"Matrix proteins are encoded by many enveloped viruses, including influenza viruses, herpes viruses and coronaviruses. Underneath the viral envelope of influenza virus, matrix protein 1 (M1) forms an oligomeric layer critical for particle stability and pH-dependent RNA genome release. However, high-resolution structures of full-length monomeric M1 and the matrix layer have not been available, impeding antiviral targeting and understanding of the pH-dependent transitions involved in cell entry. Here, purification and extensive mutagenesis revealed protein-protein interfaces required for the formation of multilayered helical M1 oligomers similar to those observed in virions exposed to the low pH of cell entry. However, single-layered helical oligomers with biochemical and ultrastructural similarity to those found in infectious virions before cell entry were observed upon mutation of a single amino acid. The highly ordered structure of the single-layered oligomers and their likeness to the matrix layer of intact virions prompted structural analysis by cryo-electron microscopy. The resulting 3.4 Å-resolution structure revealed the molecular details of M1 folding and its organization within the single-shelled matrix. The solution of the full-length M1 structure, the identification of critical assembly interfaces, and the development of M1 assembly assays with purified proteins are crucial advances for antiviral targeting of influenza viruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-24T17:15:40.028Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/87pvmycwfm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/87pvmycwfm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Selzer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhaoming","last_name":"Su","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Grigore","last_name":"Pintilie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wah","last_name":"Chiu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karla","last_name":"Kirkegaard","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Protein Assembly","Cryo-Electron Microscopy","Matrix Protein","Influenza Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8c26kkrfpr","title":"High-throughput prediction of MHC Class I and Class II neoantigens with MHCnuggets","description":"Supplementary Data S1-S6. Each data file contain per peptide raw prediction value of all tested methods. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-01T20:32:01.993Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8c26kkrfpr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8c26kkrfpr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaoshan","last_name":"Shao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachel","last_name":"Karchin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Major Histocompatibility Complex","Antigen Binding","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3nbg93bkbf","title":"Avian influenza A(H10Nx) infections in humans in China, 2013–2025: epidemiology, phylogenetics, and mammalian adaptive evolution","description":"Table 1 Clinical characteristics of human infections with avian influenza A H10Nx virus, China, 2013-2025.\n\nTable 2 Important mammalian adapting molecular markers from eight human-origin H10Nx isolates.\n\nFigure 1. Geographic and temporal distribution of human H10Nx infections in China, 2013-2025.\n\n\nFigure 2. Phylogenetic relationships of the HA and NA genes of human-origin H10Nx viruses.\n\n\nFigure 3. Amino acid conservation and frequency of key PB2 residues in avian H10Nx viruses.\n\n\nFigure 4. Global distribution, host range, and key PB2 residues of mammalian H10 subtype AIVs detected from 1984 to 2025.\n\n\nSupplementary Figure 1. Phylogenetic tree of the PB2 and PB1 genes of human-origin H10Nx avian influenza viruses.\nSupplementary Figure 2. Phylogenetic tree of the PA and NP genes of human-origin H10Nx avian influenza viruses.\n\nSupplementary Figure 3. Phylogenetic tree of the MP and NS genes of human-origin H10Nx avian influenza viruses.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-30T06:59:24.528Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3nbg93bkbf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3nbg93bkbf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Epidemiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cmghb83rmm","title":"Blockade of Endothelin-1 Receptor B Regulates Molecules of the Major Histocompatibility Complex in Sickle Cell Disease","description":"Molecules of the Major Histocompatibility Complex (MHC) have been proposed in the pathophysiology of immune and vascular alterations leading to vaso-occlusive crises in Sickle Cell Disease (SCD). Endothelial cells express MHC molecules following exposure to cytokines. SCD is characterized, in part, by vascular endothelial cell activation, increased oxidative stress, sickle cell adhesion, and excess levels of the potent mitogen, endothelin-1 (ET-1). ET-1 activates endothelial cells, induces oxidative stress and inflammation in the vascular wall, and regulates erythrocyte homeostasis. However, the role of ET-1 on MHC regulation in SCD is unclear. We first studied two sickle transgenic knockout mouse models of moderate to severe disease phenotype, βSAntilles and Berkeley (BERK) mice, respectively. We observed significant increases in MHC molecule, H2-Aa mRNA levels in spleens, lungs and kidneys from transgenic sickle mice when compared to transgenic knockout mice expressing human hemoglobin A (HbA). Mice treated for 14 days with ET-1 receptor antagonists had a significant reduction in H2-Aa mRNA. Then we characterized the effect of ET-1 on MHC class II expression in the human endothelial cell line EA.hy926. We observed dose-dependent increases in the expression of MHC class II (HLA-DR) and MHC transcription factor (CIITA) that were significantly blocked by pre-incubation of cells with BQ788, a selective blocker of ET-1 type B receptors. Chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) studies in EA.hy926 cells showed that ET-1 increased Histone H3 acetylation of the promoter region within; an event that was likewise blocked by BQ788. These results implicate ET-1 as a novel regulator of MHC class II molecules and suggest that ET-1 receptor blockade represents a promising therapeutic approach to regulate both immune and vascular responses in SCD.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-15T08:12:25.3Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cmghb83rmm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cmghb83rmm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yaritza","last_name":"Inostroza-Nieves","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Endothelin","Sickle Cell Disease","Major Histocompatibility Complex"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xg6twkvr5w","title":"ATR-FTIR spectral data of respiratory viral proteins","description":"This dataset contains basic information and ATR-FTIR spectral data for 50 recombinant respiratory viral proteins. They cover the most prevalent respiratory viruses, including human coronaviruses (four SARS-CoV-2 variants, MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV, and the common cold coronaviruses ), as well as five influenza virus strains, respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), human parainfluenza virus (HPIV), human adenovirus (HAdV) and human metapneumovirus (HMPV). From these viruses, 50 functionally important proteins are selected, such as the spike (S), receptor-binding domain (RBD), S1, S2 and nucleocapsid (N) proteins of coronaviruses, and the hemagglutinin (HA), neuraminidase (NA) and nucleoprotein (NP) of influenza viruses. IR spectra of the protein solutions were acquired using a Bruker Vertex 70v FTIR spectrometer equipped with a diamond ATR module. Spectra were recorded over the range of 4000-400 cm-1 at a resolution of 2 cm-1, with 64 scans per measurement. One to five independent replicate spectra were collected for each protein.\nThe dataset is provided as an Excel file containing three worksheets: (1) protein information, including protein name, virus origin, concentration, purity, buffer composition, and PDB reference identifiers; (2) protein spectral data; (3) buffer spectra (the corresponding raw spectra of the buffer solutions used for each protein). All spectra are presented as absorbance vs. wavenumber. The data can be used for spectral library construction, secondary structure analysis, or comparative structural studies of viral proteins.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-23T15:40:05.895Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xg6twkvr5w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xg6twkvr5w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhouzhuo","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jing","last_name":"Ni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zihao","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hao","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xia","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Infrared Spectroscopy","Respiratory Agent","Viral Protein"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5cdnt4t7xz","title":"Targeting mutant NPM1-expressing AML with T-cell receptor-like antibodies","description":"Abstract\nMutant NPM1 protein accumulation is a hallmark of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Although intracellular proteins are generally inaccessible to antibody-based therapies, proteasomal degradation of mutant NPM1 results in the generation of peptides on the tumor cell surface via major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I molecules. Enhanced presentation of NPM1 mutant A-derived peptides (NPM1mutA283-291-CLAVEEVSL) on HLA-A*02:01-positive tumor cells distinguishes malignant cells from healthy cells. Here, we report the development of an antibody, 2E2, that specifically targets the NPM1 mutant A peptide bound to HLA-A*02:01. We modified 2E2, including chimeric antibodies and bispecific antibodies. Modified antibodies selectively recognize mutant NPM1-expressing cells and mediate antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) in vitro. Moreover, bispecific antibodies derived from 2E2 redirect CD3+ T cells and NK cells to mutant NPM1-expressing cells, suppressing tumor growth both in vitro and in vivo. These findings underscore the potential of NPM1 peptide‒MHC complexes as novel immunotherapeutic targets in AML.\n\nKeywords: TCR-like antibody, Acute myeloid leukemia, Tumor immunotherapy, NPM1, HLA","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-27T15:21:17.055Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5cdnt4t7xz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5cdnt4t7xz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jianda","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Joint Funds for the Innovation of Science and Technology, Fujian Province","grant_id":"2021Y9086, 2022Y4013, 2023Y9153"},{"id":null,"name":"Special Fund from Fujian Provincial Department of Finance","grant_id":"CZBZ202301"},{"id":null,"name":"Natural Science Foundation of Fujian Province","grant_id":"2024J01583, 2022J01240"},{"id":null,"name":"the Talent Scientific Research Project of the First Affiliated Hospital of Fujian Medical University","grant_id":"YJRC4415"},{"id":null,"name":"Scientific Research Foundation of State Key Laboratory of Vaccines for Infectious Diseases, Xiang An Biomedicine Laboratory","grant_id":"2023XAKJ0102057"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82470173, U23A20419, U2005204"},{"id":null,"name":"the Project of Fujian Province Department of Science & Technology","grant_id":"2023Y9079"}],"categories":["Immunotherapy of Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tmjwhksm6y","title":"Patterns of immunogenetic and malarial parasite diversity in three species of Vietnamese passerines","description":"Understanding how genetic diversity in host immune genes affects susceptibility to pathogen infections is crucial for deciphering host-pathogen coevolutionary dynamics. In this study, we investigated the genetic diversity of mitochondrial cytochrome b (cyt b) and key immune genes including Toll-like receptors (TLR3 exon 4 and TLR4 exon 3) and the major histocompatibility complex (MHC class I exon 3) in three sympatric passerine bird species (Arachnothera longirostra, Copsychus malabaricus, and Pitta moluccensis) from Southeast Asia, includinh their associations with Plasmodium infections. Our cyt b analyses indicated that A. longirostra experienced past population expansions, evidenced by significantly low Tajima's D and Fu's Fs values, whereas C. malabaricus and P. moluccensis maintained relatively constant effective population sizes. Immune gene diversity revealed unique profiles across the three species: MHC class I genes displayed positive Tajima's D values, suggesting balancing selection and possible historical bottlenecks, particularly in A. longirostra and P. moluccensis. Selection analyses showed that MHC genes are primarily under purifying selection with species-specific codons under diversifying selection. TLR genes were predominantly governed by purifying selection, with minor instances of diversifying selection noted. Generalized linear models demonstrated that the total number of MHC supertypes did not significantly influence Plasmodium infection status across passerine species, providing no support for the heterozygote advantage hypothesis. However, in P. moluccensis, specific MHC supertypes were significantly associated with Plasmodium operational taxonomic units (OTUs), indicating a possible rare-allele advantage in MHC-mediated malaria resistance. These findings highlight the complex interplay between host genetic diversity and parasite infection, suggesting that balancing selection on immune genes may vary even among sympatric species facing similar pathogen pressures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-25T16:03:08.743Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tmjwhksm6y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tmjwhksm6y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dragomir","last_name":"Damnjanovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Masoud","last_name":"Nazarizadeh","orcid":"0000-0002-1891-9870"},{"first_name":"Milena","last_name":"Nováková","orcid":"0000-0002-9711-253X"},{"first_name":"Oleg","last_name":"Tolstenkov","orcid":"0000-0002-6484-9965"},{"first_name":"Anvar","last_name":"Kerimov","orcid":"0000-0002-3543-2757"},{"first_name":"Andrey","last_name":"Bushuev","orcid":"0000-0003-0230-7420"},{"first_name":"Jan","last_name":"Štefka","orcid":"0000-0002-1283-9730"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Immunology","Parasitology","Ornithology","Evolutionary Selection","Plasmodium"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4bk7w8298s","title":"Protocol for purification and identification of MHC class I immunopeptidome ","description":"A detailed protocol for purification and identification of MHC class I peptides including in-house purification of anti-MHC-antibody from hybridoma","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-27T16:39:26.887Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4bk7w8298s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4bk7w8298s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sonali","last_name":"Mohan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keshava","last_name":"Datta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rebekah","last_name":"Ziegman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Corey","last_name":"Smith","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Harsha ","last_name":"Gowda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f5y9cggnxy","title":"k-mers 1D and 2D representation dataset of SARS-CoV-2 nucleotide sequences","description":"The dataset provides five types of k-mers genome representation characterized as k-mers count 1D, k-mers probability 1D, k-mers count 2D, k-mers probability 2D, and k-mers image. The dataset is composed of 1557 virus instances of SARS-CoV-2. Besides, the dataset also provides a data stream of 11540 viruses from the Virus-Host DB dataset and the other three Riboviria viruses from NCBI (Betacoronavirus RaTG13, bat-SL-CoVZC45, and bat-SL-CoVZXC21).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-26T17:17:27.893Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f5y9cggnxy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f5y9cggnxy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Raquel de M.","last_name":"Barbosa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marcelo A. C.","last_name":"Fernandes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Digital Signal Processing","Computational Bioinformatics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4mpjm3fnzr","title":"Patterns of immunogenetic and malarial parasite diversity in three species of Vietnamese passerines","description":"Understanding how genetic diversity in host immune genes affects susceptibility to pathogen infections is crucial for deciphering host-pathogen coevolutionary dynamics. In this study, we investigated the genetic diversity of mitochondrial cytochrome b (cyt b) and key immune genes including Toll-like receptors (TLR3 exon 4 and TLR4 exon 3) and the major histocompatibility complex (MHC class I exon 3) in three sympatric passerine bird species (Arachnothera longirostra, Copsychus malabaricus, and Pitta moluccensis) from Southeast Asia, including their associations with Plasmodium infections. Our cyt b analyses indicated that A. longirostra experienced past population expansions, evidenced by significantly low Tajima's D and Fu's Fs values, whereas C. malabaricus and P. moluccensis maintained relatively constant effective population sizes. Immune gene diversity revealed unique profiles across the three species: MHC class I genes displayed positive Tajima's D values, suggesting balancing selection and possible historical bottlenecks, particularly in A. longirostra and P. moluccensis. Selection analyses showed that MHC genes are primarily under purifying selection with species-specific codons under diversifying selection. TLR genes were predominantly governed by purifying selection, with minor instances of diversifying selection noted. Generalized linear models demonstrated that the total number of MHC supertypes did not significantly influence Plasmodium infection status across passerine species, providing no support for the heterozygote advantage hypothesis. However, in P. moluccensis, specific MHC supertypes were significantly associated with Plasmodium operational taxonomic units (OTUs), indicating a possible rare-allele advantage in MHC-mediated malaria resistance. These findings highlight the complex interplay between host genetic diversity and parasite infection, suggesting that balancing selection on immune genes may vary even among sympatric species facing similar pathogen pressures.\n\nNotice: This dataset is derived from the sequencing data previously deposited for the study ‘Hidden Plasmodium diversity revealed in southeastern Asian passerines using next-generation amplicon sequencing’ and contains processed/reorganized data used for the present analysis.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-29T09:47:00.64Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mpjm3fnzr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mpjm3fnzr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dragomir","last_name":"Damnjanovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Masoud","last_name":"Nazarizadeh","orcid":"0000-0002-1891-9870"},{"first_name":"Milena","last_name":"Novakova","orcid":"0000-0002-9711-253X"},{"first_name":"Oleg","last_name":"Tolstenkov","orcid":"0000-0002-6484-9965"},{"first_name":"Jan","last_name":"Štefka","orcid":"0000-0002-1283-9730"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Immunology","Parasitology","Ornithology","Evolutionary Selection"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122139,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":110,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122139,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121682,"restricted":61,"embargoed":396},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2041},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1344},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":552},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":443},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":382},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":339},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":280},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":245}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119569},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1891},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":126},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":69},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":60},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":873},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":776},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":401},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":384},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":359}]}}